{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/q5wze5cz","slideIndex":13,"position":{"left":68,"top":218,"width":1145,"height":304},"text":"BEGIN;\nUPDATE FoodDonations SET status = 'CLAIMED'\nWHERE donation_id = ? AND status = 'AVAILABLE'\n  AND pickup_deadline > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP;\n-- Continue only when affected rows = 1\nINSERT INTO DonationClaims (...) VALUES (...);\nCOMMIT;","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":27,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#194D3B","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/r65g7adk","slideIndex":13,"position":{"left":68,"top":553,"width":1130,"height":65},"text":"If the update changes zero rows, or the insert fails, roll back the transaction.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Aptos","color":"#253C33","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/7r4kt9","slideIndex":13,"text":"\nSource: FoodBridge project brief supplied by the team. Technical structures and examples describe a proposed design, not verified implementation.\nThis is illustrative transaction pseudocode. Implement the unit of work with a Spring @Transactional service and MySQL InnoDB. Check the update count before insertion. Under concurrent claims, the conditional update allows only an eligible available row to change. UNIQUE(donation_id) supplies an additional safeguard. Roll back on failure. Atomicity keeps the steps together, consistency enforces rules, isolation coordinates concurrent work, and durability preserves committed changes."}
